# 15. PLATFORM CONFIGURATION MANAGEMENT

## 15.1 Objective

Create a centralized location for approved platform-level configuration.

This module must manage configurable application behavior without requiring source-code changes for every routine administrative setting.

## 15.2 Configuration Categories

Potential categories include:

### General Settings

- Platform display name.
- Support contact details.
- Business contact information.
- Default display preferences.

### Estimate Settings

- Estimate validity period.
- Approved rounding configuration.
- Estimate display options.

### Upload Settings

- Allowed image formats.
- Maximum image size.
- Maximum images per deck.

Only expose settings that the existing application can safely enforce.

### Notification Settings

- Notification preferences.
- Enabled notification categories.
- Approved notification templates, if supported.

### Business Settings

- Approved currency configuration.
- Business timezone.
- Regional formatting.
- Approved operational settings.

### Security Settings

- Existing supported authentication settings.
- Existing rate-limit configuration where safely configurable.
- Session policy settings only if implemented and approved.

## 15.3 Configuration CRUD

Admin must be able to:

- View configuration.
- Create configuration entries where supported.
- Update configuration values.
- Activate or deactivate configurable options.
- View configuration history.

Do not allow arbitrary configuration keys or unrestricted JSON values.

Every configurable field must have:

- A defined type.
- Validation.
- Allowed range or values.
- Default behavior.
- Backend enforcement.

## 15.4 Configuration Security

Never store secrets in ordinary application configuration records.

Examples of secrets:

- Database credentials.
- JWT secrets.
- API private keys.
- Cloud storage secrets.
- SMTP passwords.

Secrets must remain in approved environment or secret-management systems.

Do not expose secrets to the frontend.

